<!-- Section 1: sec-intro -->
<section id="sec-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Right Triangle Inradius Calculator</h2>
  <p>Find the inradius of a right triangle from its two legs and hypotenuse.</p>
  <p>The inradius is the radius of the incircle, the circle that fits inside the triangle and touches all three sides. The calculator needs three numbers: leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, and hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, where <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> are the sides that form the right angle and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the side opposite it. The formula is <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{a+b-c}{2}]]')} />, where <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> is the inradius. It adds the two legs, subtracts the hypotenuse, and divides the result by two.</p>
  <p>For example, a 6-8-10 triangle gives <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{6+8-10}{2} = 2]]')} />. That 2 is the radius of the circle inside the triangle, measured in the same unit as the sides. The circle touches each side at exactly one point, and its center, the incenter, sits the same distance from all three sides.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: sec-calculate -->
<section id="sec-calculate" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Calculate the Inradius of a Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>The calculator on this page has three input fields, one for each side of the triangle, and one result. Enter the three side lengths, all in the same unit, then click Calculate Inradius r. The result is a length, the radius of the incircle, given in that same unit.</p>
  <p>Leg a is the first side that forms the right angle, and leg b is the second. It does not matter which leg you call <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and which you call <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, because the formula adds them together. The hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the side opposite the right angle, and it must be the longest side of the triangle. If the number entered for <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is shorter than a leg, the three lengths cannot form a right triangle, and the result will not be meaningful.</p>
  <p>Keep the units the same across all three fields. If <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> are in centimeters, <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> must be in centimeters too, and the answer comes out in centimeters. Read the answer as a radius, not a diameter: a radius runs from the center of the circle to its edge, while a diameter runs all the way across. Use Reset to clear the fields before starting a new problem.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 3: sec-what-inradius -->
<section id="sec-what-inradius" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is the Inradius of a Right Triangle?</h2>
  <p>The inradius of a right triangle is the radius of its incircle, the largest circle that fits inside the triangle. Picture triangle ABC with the right angle at C, leg AC called <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, leg BC called <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, and hypotenuse AB called <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />. Now draw the biggest circle you can inside that triangle without crossing any side. That circle touches all three sides, and its radius is the inradius, written <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} />.</p>
  <p>The center of the incircle is called the incenter, written <span set:html={rich('[[I]]')} />. The incenter sits at the same perpendicular distance from all three sides, and that distance is <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} />. Each radius drawn from <span set:html={rich('[[I]]')} /> to a side meets that side at a right angle, which is why the circle only just touches the side instead of crossing it. The incircle stays inside the triangle because every point of the circle is at distance <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> or less from <span set:html={rich('[[I]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[I]]')} /> itself is inside the triangle.</p>
  <p>A small example makes the idea concrete. In a 3-4-5 triangle, the inradius is <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{3+4-5}{2} = 1]]')} />. The incircle has a radius of 1 unit, so its center sits 1 unit away from each side. The <a href="/right-triangle-radius-calculator/">right triangle radius calculator</a> covers both radii of a right triangle on one page, if you want to compare the incircle with the circle through the vertices.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('inradius-main-incircle.svg')} alt="Right triangle ABC with the incircle inside it, center I, and radius r touching all three sides" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Triangle ABC with its incircle: center I sits at distance r from each side.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: sec-incircle -->
<section id="sec-incircle" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is an Incircle in Right Triangle Geometry?</h2>
  <p>An incircle is a circle drawn entirely inside a triangle so that it touches, or is tangent to, all three sides. Each side of the triangle is a tangent line to the circle: it meets the circle at exactly one point and does not cut through it. Those three meeting points are called the points of tangency, one on each side.</p>
  <p>The radius drawn from the incenter to a point of tangency is always perpendicular to that side. This is a general fact about circles: a radius to a point of tangency meets the tangent line at a right angle. Because the incenter is the same distance from all three sides, the three radii to the three points of tangency all have the same length <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} />, so a single circle fits all three sides at once.</p>
  <p>Every triangle has exactly one incircle. The three angle bisectors of a triangle meet at one point, the incenter, and the distance from that point to the sides fixes the radius. A second, different circle could not touch all three sides from inside, because its center would have to be equally distant from all three sides too, and only the incenter has that property. Note that the incircle is not the circle through the vertices: it touches the sides from inside and never reaches a vertex.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('inradius-tangency-points.svg')} alt="Incircle touching all three sides of a right triangle at three points of tangency with perpendicular radii" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Three points of tangency: each radius meets its side at 90 degrees.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 5: sec-incenter -->
<section id="sec-incenter" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Incenter of a Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>The incenter of a triangle is the point where its three internal angle bisectors meet. An angle bisector is a line that splits an angle into two equal halves. Draw the bisector of angle A, the bisector of angle B, and the bisector of angle C, and all three cross at a single point. That point is the incenter <span set:html={rich('[[I]]')} />, and it is the center of the incircle.</p>
  <p>The incenter is equally distant from all three sides because of how angle bisectors behave. Every point on the bisector of an angle is the same distance from the two sides forming that angle. A point on two bisectors at once is therefore the same distance from all three sides, and the incenter lies on all three bisectors. That common distance is the inradius <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} />, so the circle centered at <span set:html={rich('[[I]]')} /> with radius <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> touches each side.</p>
  <p>For a right triangle with the right angle at C, the incenter lies along the bisector of the 90-degree angle, which runs diagonally across the corner at C at 45 degrees to each leg. The incenter always lies inside the triangle, never on a side or outside it, because the three bisectors meet in the interior. That is why the incircle fits inside the triangle rather than spilling out of it.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('inradius-angle-bisectors.svg')} alt="Right triangle with all three internal angle bisectors meeting at the incenter I" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">The three angle bisectors meet at I, the incenter, at distance r from each side.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: sec-formula -->
<section id="sec-formula" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Right Triangle Inradius Formula</h2>
  <p>The formula for the inradius of a right triangle is short enough to remember at a glance:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[r = \\frac{a+b-c}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Here <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> is the inradius, <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is the first leg, <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> is the second leg, and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the hypotenuse. The formula works in three stages, and each stage has a clear meaning.</p>
  <p>Stage 1: add the two legs, <span set:html={rich('[[a+b]]')} />. This combined length is always longer than the hypotenuse, because the two legs together stretch further than the straight line across. Stage 2: subtract the hypotenuse, <span set:html={rich('[[a+b-c]]')} />. This leaves the amount by which the legs exceed the hypotenuse. Stage 3: divide by two, <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{a+b-c}{2}]]')} />. The division turns that leftover length into the radius of the incircle, and the derivation section below shows why halving is exactly right.</p>
  <p>Take the 6-8-10 triangle: <span set:html={rich('[[a+b = 14]]')} />, then <span set:html={rich('[[14-10 = 4]]')} />, then <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{4}{2} = 2]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[r = 2]]')} />. The formula is specific to right triangles. For a triangle that is not right-angled, the inradius still equals area divided by semiperimeter, <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{A}{s}]]')} />, but the shortcut <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{a+b-c}{2}]]')} /> does not apply, because its proof depends on the right angle.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('inradius-formula.svg')} alt="Right triangle labeled with legs a and b, hypotenuse c, incenter I, and inradius r beside the formula r equals a plus b minus c over 2" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">The formula uses all three sides: add the legs, subtract the hypotenuse, divide by two.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 7: sec-why-formula-works -->
<section id="sec-why-formula-works" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Does the Right Triangle Inradius Formula Work?</h2>
  <p>The formula comes from a simple fact about tangents: the two tangent segments drawn from the same point outside a circle to that circle have equal lengths. Each vertex of the triangle sits outside the incircle, and the two sides meeting at that vertex are both tangent to the circle. So the two tangent segments from each vertex are equal.</p>
  <p>Start at the right-angle vertex C. The two sides meeting at C are the legs, and the incircle touches each leg at a point that is distance <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> from C along the side. This is because the two radii from the incenter to the legs, together with the two short segments along the legs, form a square of side <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> at the corner C. So the tangent segment from C along either leg has length <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} />.</p>
  <p>Now look at the other two vertices. Let <span set:html={rich('[[x]]')} /> be the tangent length from vertex A along the sides, and let <span set:html={rich('[[y]]')} /> be the tangent length from vertex B. At vertex A, one tangent runs along leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> toward C and the other runs along the hypotenuse toward B, so both have length <span set:html={rich('[[x]]')} />. At vertex B, both tangents have length <span set:html={rich('[[y]]')} />. The side lengths are then built from these pieces:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a = r + x]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b = r + y]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = x + y]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Add the first two equations: <span set:html={rich('[[a+b = 2r+x+y]]')} />. Since <span set:html={rich('[[x+y = c]]')} />, this becomes <span set:html={rich('[[a+b = 2r+c]]')} />. Rearranging gives <span set:html={rich('[[2r = a+b-c]]')} />, and dividing by two gives the formula, <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{a+b-c}{2}]]')} />. Every step follows from equal tangent lengths, so the formula is not a trick to memorize, it is a direct result of how the incircle touches the sides.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 8: sec-how-to-use -->
<section id="sec-how-to-use" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the Right Triangle Inradius Calculator</h2>
  <p>Fourteen short steps take you from a labeled triangle to a checked answer. The steps in order are:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify the right triangle in your problem and confirm it has a right angle.</li>
    <li>Locate the right-angle vertex, usually marked with a small square, and call it C.</li>
    <li>Identify the first perpendicular leg a, one of the two sides forming the right angle.</li>
    <li>Identify the second perpendicular leg b, the other side forming the right angle.</li>
    <li>Identify the complete hypotenuse c, the side opposite the right angle.</li>
    <li>Confirm that c is the longest side. If it is not, relabel the diagram before continuing.</li>
    <li>Check that the three sides satisfy the Pythagorean theorem, <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2 = c^2]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Select compatible measurement units and convert any measurement that uses a different unit.</li>
    <li>Enter leg a in the Leg a field.</li>
    <li>Enter leg b in the Leg b field.</li>
    <li>Enter hypotenuse c in the Hypotenuse c field.</li>
    <li>Click Calculate Inradius r and read the result.</li>
    <li>Check the result: it must be positive, in the same unit as the sides, and smaller than either leg.</li>
    <li>Use Reset when starting another calculation.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Entering the wrong side as <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the error that causes the most trouble. If a leg is entered in the hypotenuse field, the formula subtracts a leg instead of the hypotenuse, and the answer is too large. Always check that the value in the hypotenuse field is the longest of the three before clicking Calculate.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 9: sec-step-example -->
<section id="sec-step-example" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Example of Finding Inradius</h2>
  <p>Use the example from the top of the page, <span set:html={rich('[[a = 6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 8]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} />. The five steps below show the full working, with nothing skipped.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write the formula: <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{a+b-c}{2}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Substitute the values: <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{6+8-10}{2}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Add the legs: <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{14-10}{2}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Subtract the hypotenuse: <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{4}{2}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Divide: <span set:html={rich('[[r = 2]]')} />.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The answer 2 means the incircle has a radius of 2 units, in the same unit as the sides. Its center sits 2 units from each side, and the circle touches each side at exactly one point.</p>
  <p>Verify that the measurements form a right triangle before trusting the answer: <span set:html={rich('[[6^2+8^2 = 36+64 = 100 = 10^2]]')} />, so the Pythagorean theorem holds and the triangle is genuinely right-angled. A second check uses area and semiperimeter, <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\frac{6 \\times 8}{2} = 24]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s = \\frac{6+8+10}{2} = 12]]')} />, giving <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{24}{12} = 2]]')} />, the same answer by a different route.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('inradius-example-6-8-10.svg')} alt="Worked example with a equals 6, b equals 8, c equals 10, and inradius r equals 2, incircle touching all three sides" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Worked example: a = 6, b = 8, c = 10 gives r = 2, checked by area and semiperimeter.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 10: sec-diagram -->
<section id="sec-diagram" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Right Triangle Incircle Diagram Explained</h2>
  <p>The diagram shows triangle ABC with the right angle at C. Side AC is leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, side BC is leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, and side AB is the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />. The point <span set:html={rich('[[I]]')} /> marks the incenter inside the triangle, and a circle centered at <span set:html={rich('[[I]]')} /> touches all three sides.</p>
  <p>Read the diagram in this order. Start at A, run along leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> down to C, then along leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> across to B, then back along the hypotenuse from B to A. The incircle sits inside this boundary, touching leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> at one point, leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> at a second point, and the hypotenuse at a third point. From <span set:html={rich('[[I]]')} />, three short segments run to those three points, and each one is a radius of length <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} />.</p>
  <p>Each radius meets its side at a right angle, which is the mark of a tangent: the side just grazes the circle at that point instead of cutting across it. All three radii have the same length, which is why one circle can touch all three sides at once. The circle does not pass through any vertex: the vertices sit outside the circle, and the tangent segments from each vertex to the circle are the lengths <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[x]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[y]]')} /> used in the derivation above.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 11: sec-area-semiperimeter -->
<section id="sec-area-semiperimeter" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate Inradius Using Area and Semiperimeter</h2>
  <p>There is a second way to find the inradius, using the area and the semiperimeter of the triangle:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[r = \\frac{A}{s}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Here <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> is the area of the triangle and <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> is the semiperimeter, half the perimeter. For a right triangle, the area is half the product of the legs, <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\frac{ab}{2}]]')} />, because the two legs act as base and height. The semiperimeter is <span set:html={rich('[[s = \\frac{a+b+c}{2}]]')} />. The <a href="/right-triangle-area-calculator/">right triangle area calculator</a> finds <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> from the two legs, and the <a href="/right-triangle-semiperimeter-calculator/">right triangle semiperimeter calculator</a> finds <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> from all three sides.</p>
  <p>Substituting the right-triangle expressions gives <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{ab/2}{(a+b+c)/2}]]')} />, and the halves cancel to leave <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{ab}{a+b+c}]]')} />. This is the equivalent side-length formula from the start of the guide, and it gives the same answer as <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{a+b-c}{2}]]')} /> for every valid right triangle, because the two expressions are equal whenever <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2 = c^2]]')} />.</p>
  <p>Use the 6-8-10 triangle to see both routes agree. The area is <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\frac{6 \\times 8}{2} = 24]]')} />, and the semiperimeter is <span set:html={rich('[[s = \\frac{6+8+10}{2} = 12]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{24}{12} = 2]]')} />. The standard formula gives the same result, <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{6+8-10}{2} = 2]]')} />. The calculator on this page uses the three-side formula, and the area route is a useful independent check.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('inradius-area-semiperimeter.svg')} alt="Right triangle divided into three smaller triangles sharing the incenter, showing area equals r times s" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Three triangles sharing I: their areas add up to A = r times s.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 12: sec-semiperimeter-relation -->
<section id="sec-semiperimeter-relation" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Relationship Between Inradius and Semiperimeter</h2>
  <p>The semiperimeter <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> is half the perimeter of the triangle:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[s = \\frac{a+b+c}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>It is called semiperimeter because semi means half: the full perimeter is <span set:html={rich('[[P = a+b+c]]')} />, and the semiperimeter is half of that. Do not confuse the two. The perimeter is the total length around the triangle, while the semiperimeter is a shorter helper value that appears in area formulas and in the inradius formula <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{A}{s}]]')} />.</p>
  <p>The semiperimeter alone cannot determine the inradius, because the formula <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{A}{s}]]')} /> also needs the area <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />. Two triangles can share the same semiperimeter and have different areas, and then their inradii differ too. The area carries the information about the shape that the semiperimeter leaves out. For the 6-8-10 triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[s = 12]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[A = 24]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{24}{12} = 2]]')} />. A different triangle with <span set:html={rich('[[s = 12]]')} /> but a smaller area would have a smaller inradius.</p>
  <p>There is a neat way to see why <span set:html={rich('[[A = rs]]')} /> holds. Join the incenter <span set:html={rich('[[I]]')} /> to the three vertices, splitting the triangle into three smaller triangles. Each small triangle has height <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> and a base equal to one side of the big triangle, so their areas are <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{ra}{2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{rb}{2}]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{rc}{2}]]')} />. Adding them gives <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\frac{r(a+b+c)}{2} = r \\times s]]')} />, since <span set:html={rich('[[s = \\frac{a+b+c}{2}]]')} />. Dividing by <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> gives <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{A}{s}]]')} />.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 13: sec-examples -->
<section id="sec-examples" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Worked Examples of Calculating Right Triangle Inradius</h2>
  <h3>Example 1: Basic 3-4-5 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 4]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 5]]')} />, the formula gives <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{3+4-5}{2} = \\frac{2}{2} = 1]]')} />. The incircle has a radius of 1 unit. Check the triangle first: <span set:html={rich('[[3^2+4^2 = 9+16 = 25 = 5^2]]')} />, so it is a valid right triangle.</p>
  <h3>Example 2: Existing 6-8-10 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 8]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{6+8-10}{2} = \\frac{4}{2} = 2]]')} />. The incircle has a radius of 2 units, twice the radius of the 3-4-5 triangle, because this triangle is the 3-4-5 triangle scaled up by a factor of 2. Check: <span set:html={rich('[[6^2+8^2 = 36+64 = 100 = 10^2]]')} />.</p>
  <h3>Example 3: 5-12-13 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 12]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 13]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{5+12-13}{2} = \\frac{4}{2} = 2]]')} />. The inradius is 2 units, the same as the 6-8-10 triangle, even though the triangle is larger and differently shaped. Check: <span set:html={rich('[[5^2+12^2 = 25+144 = 169 = 13^2]]')} />.</p>
  <h3>Example 4: 8-15-17 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 8]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 15]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 17]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{8+15-17}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3]]')} />. The incircle has a radius of 3 units. Check: <span set:html={rich('[[8^2+15^2 = 64+225 = 289 = 17^2]]')} />.</p>
  <h3>Example 5: Larger Measurements</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 9]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 12]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 15]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{9+12-15}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3]]')} />. This is the 3-4-5 triangle scaled by 3, so the inradius is 3 times the 3-4-5 inradius of 1. Check: <span set:html={rich('[[9^2+12^2 = 81+144 = 225 = 15^2]]')} />.</p>
  <h3>Example 6: Decimal Measurements</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 4.5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 6]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 7.5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{4.5+6-7.5}{2} = \\frac{3}{2} = 1.5]]')} />. The inradius is 1.5 units. Decimals change nothing about the method: add, subtract, divide by two. Check: <span set:html={rich('[[4.5^2+6^2 = 20.25+36 = 56.25 = 7.5^2]]')} />.</p>
  <h3>Example 7: Different Units</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 30]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 40]]')} /> cm, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 0.5]]')} /> m, convert the hypotenuse first: 0.5 m equals 50 cm, because 1 m is 100 cm. Now all three sides use centimeters, so <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{30+40-50}{2} = \\frac{20}{2} = 10]]')} /> cm. Check: <span set:html={rich('[[30^2+40^2 = 900+1600 = 2500 = 50^2]]')} />. Mixing meters and centimeters without converting would give a wrong answer, so always convert first.</p>
  <h3>Example 8: Larger Decimal Values</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 7.2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 9.6]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 12]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{7.2+9.6-12}{2} = \\frac{4.8}{2} = 2.4]]')} />. The inradius is 2.4 units. This is the 3-4-5 triangle scaled by 2.4, so the inradius is 2.4 times the 3-4-5 inradius of 1. Check: <span set:html={rich('[[7.2^2+9.6^2 = 51.84+92.16 = 144 = 12^2]]')} />.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 14: sec-legs-known -->
<section id="sec-legs-known" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate Inradius When Only the Two Legs Are Known</h2>
  <p>The calculator on this page requires all three side lengths, so it cannot run on the two legs alone. If only <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> are known, find the hypotenuse first with the Pythagorean theorem, then use the inradius formula. The two steps are:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = \\sqrt{a^2+b^2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[r = \\frac{a+b-c}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[a = 6]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b = 8]]')} />. First calculate the hypotenuse: <span set:html={rich('[[c = \\sqrt{36+64} = \\sqrt{100} = 10]]')} />. Then calculate the inradius: <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{6+8-10}{2} = 2]]')} />. The <a href="/right-triangle-side-calculator/">right triangle side calculator</a> finds the missing hypotenuse from two legs, which is the same first step used here, and the <a href="/pythagorean-theorem-calculator/">Pythagorean theorem calculator</a> does the same calculation directly. Do not skip the first step: entering a leg in the hypotenuse field gives a wrong inradius, as the mistakes section below shows.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 15: sec-vs-circumradius -->
<section id="sec-vs-circumradius" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Relationship Between Inradius and Circumradius</h2>
  <p>The inradius <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> and the circumradius <span set:html={rich('[[R]]')} /> are the two radii of a right triangle, and they describe two different circles. The inradius is the radius of the incircle, the circle inside the triangle that touches all three sides. The circumradius is the radius of the circumcircle, the circle outside the triangle that passes through all three vertices. One circle sits inside the triangle, the other surrounds it.</p>
  <p>For a right triangle, the circumradius is <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} />, because the hypotenuse is the diameter of the circumcircle. The inradius is <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{a+b-c}{2}]]')} />, which needs all three sides, because how closely the incircle fits depends on the shape of the triangle, not only on its longest side. Use the 6-8-10 triangle to see the difference in numbers: <span set:html={rich('[[r = 2]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[R = 5]]')} />.</p>
  <p>The centers differ too. The incenter lies inside the triangle, where the angle bisectors meet, at distance <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> from each side. The circumcenter lies at the midpoint of the hypotenuse, on the boundary of the triangle, at distance <span set:html={rich('[[R]]')} /> from each vertex. For problems about the circumcircle, use the <a href="/right-triangle-circumradius-calculator/">right triangle circumradius calculator</a>, which applies <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} /> directly.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm my-6 border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Feature</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Inradius r</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Circumradius R</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Circle</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Incircle</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Circumcircle</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Position</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Inside the triangle</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Around the triangle</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Contact</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Tangent to sides</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Passes through vertices</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Right triangle formula</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">(a+b-c)/2</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">c/2</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Center</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Incenter</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Circumcenter</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">6-8-10 value</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">2</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">5</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
