</section>

<!-- Section 16: sec-verify -->
<section id="sec-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Verify Your Inradius Calculation</h2>
  <p>Four independent checks catch almost every error. Run at least one on every answer, and all four when the problem matters. The example below uses the 6-8-10 triangle, with <span set:html={rich('[[r = 2]]')} />, throughout.</p>
  <h3>Check 1: Original Formula</h3>
  <p>Recompute with <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{a+b-c}{2}]]')} /> from scratch, without looking at your first working. Here <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{6+8-10}{2} = 2]]')} />. A fresh calculation catches slips in the first one, especially a subtraction done in the wrong order.</p>
  <h3>Check 2: Area and Semiperimeter</h3>
  <p>Verify with <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{A}{s}]]')} />. Here <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\frac{6 \\times 8}{2} = 24]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s = \\frac{6+8+10}{2} = 12]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{24}{12} = 2]]')} />. This route uses multiplication and division instead of addition and subtraction, so it catches a different class of error.</p>
  <h3>Check 3: Pythagorean Theorem</h3>
  <p>Verify <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2 = c^2]]')} />. Here <span set:html={rich('[[6^2+8^2 = 36+64 = 100 = 10^2]]')} />, so the three sides form a genuine right triangle. If this check fails, the inradius formula does not apply at all, and no value of <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> from it can be trusted.</p>
  <h3>Check 4: Geometric Reasonableness</h3>
  <p>Confirm that <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> is positive, smaller than either leg, and in the correct unit. Here 2 is positive and smaller than both 6 and 8, and the unit matches the sides. The circle must fit inside the triangle, so an inradius larger than the shortest leg is impossible and signals an error, usually a leg entered as the hypotenuse.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 17: sec-properties -->
<section id="sec-properties" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Important Properties of the Inradius in a Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>Nine properties follow directly from the formula and the geometry of the incircle. Each one doubles as a sense check on a new answer.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The inradius is always positive for a valid, nondegenerate triangle, because <span set:html={rich('[[a+b]]')} /> is always greater than <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />.</li>
    <li>The incenter lies inside the triangle, where the three angle bisectors meet.</li>
    <li>The inradius is the shortest perpendicular distance from the incenter to each side, and all three distances are equal to <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} />.</li>
    <li>The incircle touches all three sides, one point of tangency on each side.</li>
    <li>Tangent segments from the same external point have equal lengths, which is the fact the formula is derived from.</li>
    <li>Increasing the overall scale of the triangle increases the inradius by the same scale factor: doubling every side doubles <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} />, and tripling every side triples <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} />.</li>
    <li>The inradius is not the same as the circumradius. In a 6-8-10 triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[r = 2]]')} /> while <span set:html={rich('[[R = 5]]')} />.</li>
    <li>The inradius depends on the shape of the triangle as well as its size: a 5-12-13 triangle and a 6-8-10 triangle both have <span set:html={rich('[[r = 2]]')} />, even though their sides differ.</li>
    <li>The inradius can be calculated from side lengths with <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{a+b-c}{2}]]')} /> or from area and semiperimeter with <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{A}{s}]]')} />, and both routes give the same answer.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<!-- Section 18: sec-mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Calculating Inradius</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Using the circumradius formula instead of the inradius formula: <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} /> gives 5 for a 6-8-10 triangle, while the inradius is 2. The two formulas answer different questions.</li>
    <li>Entering c as a leg rather than the hypotenuse: if 8 is entered as c and 10 as b, the formula gives <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{6+10-8}{2} = 4]]')} />, not 2. Always put the longest side in the hypotenuse field.</li>
    <li>Forgetting to subtract c: adding the legs and dividing by two, <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{6+8}{2} = 7]]')} />, gives a number far larger than the true inradius of 2.</li>
    <li>Forgetting to divide by two: <span set:html={rich('[[6+8-10 = 4]]')} /> is twice the inradius. The division by two is part of the formula, not an optional final step.</li>
    <li>Entering invalid side measurements: sides of 3, 4, and 6 do not satisfy <span set:html={rich('[[3^2+4^2 = 6^2]]')} />, since 25 does not equal 36, so the inradius formula does not apply to them.</li>
    <li>Ignoring the Pythagorean relationship: any three lengths can be fed into the formula, but only a genuine right triangle gives a meaningful inradius. Check <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2 = c^2]]')} /> first.</li>
    <li>Mixing measurement units: legs in centimeters and a hypotenuse in meters produce a wrong answer. Convert every measurement to one unit before calculating.</li>
    <li>Confusing inradius with diameter: the inradius is the distance from the center to the edge. The diameter of the incircle is twice the inradius, so for a 6-8-10 triangle it is 4, not 2.</li>
    <li>Confusing incenter with circumcenter: the incenter is inside the triangle where the angle bisectors meet, while the circumcenter is the midpoint of the hypotenuse. They are different points.</li>
    <li>Assuming the incircle passes through the triangle's vertices: the incircle touches the sides and never reaches a vertex. The circle through the vertices is the circumcircle.</li>
    <li>Assuming the radius is measured along a side rather than perpendicular to it: the inradius is the perpendicular distance from the incenter to a side, which is the shortest distance to that side.</li>
    <li>Rounding intermediate calculations too early: keep full precision through the addition and subtraction, and round only the final answer. Early rounding shifts the result.</li>
    <li>Using only two side lengths without calculating the hypotenuse first: the formula needs all three sides. Find <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> with the Pythagorean theorem before using it.</li>
    <li>Confusing perimeter with semiperimeter: the semiperimeter is half the perimeter, <span set:html={rich('[[s = \\frac{a+b+c}{2}]]')} />. Using the full perimeter in <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{A}{s}]]')} /> halves the correct answer.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<!-- Section 19: sec-applications -->
<section id="sec-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Practical Applications of Right Triangle Inradius</h2>
  <p>The most direct use is geometry homework and classroom work, where inradius problems test whether students can identify the hypotenuse, apply <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{a+b-c}{2}]]')} />, and check the result with area and semiperimeter. Circle construction exercises use the same idea: given a right triangle, draw its incircle by finding the incenter where the angle bisectors meet and setting a compass to the inradius.</p>
  <p>Technical drawing uses the incircle when a circle must fit exactly inside a triangular frame or when a rounded corner must touch two edges of a shape. Geometric modeling and coordinate geometry use the incenter as the point inside a triangle that is equally distant from all three sides, which is the natural center for an inscribed shape.</p>
  <p>Teachers use the topic as a bridge lesson between triangles and circles, because the incircle is one of the first places where the two subjects meet. Tangent lengths, angle bisectors, area, and semiperimeter all appear in a single picture, so the inradius is a compact way to review them together. Real design projects that need an inscribed circle also need tolerances and material limits beyond this page, so treat the result as the exact geometric value and add practical margins separately.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 20: sec-practice -->
<section id="sec-practice" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Practice Problems With Answers</h2>
  <p>Try all six problems on paper before reading the answers. None of the values repeat the worked examples, and every solution is worked out in full below.</p>
  <h3>Problem 1: Basic Whole Number</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has legs <span set:html={rich('[[a = 20]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[b = 21]]')} /> cm and hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c = 29]]')} /> cm. Find the inradius <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} />.</p>
  <h3>Problem 2: Decimal Measurement</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 10.5]]')} /> m, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 14]]')} /> m, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 17.5]]')} /> m, find the inradius <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> in meters.</p>
  <h3>Problem 3: Unit Conversion</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has legs <span set:html={rich('[[a = 1.5]]')} /> m and <span set:html={rich('[[b = 2]]')} /> m and hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c = 250]]')} /> cm. Find the inradius in meters.</p>
  <h3>Problem 4: Find the Hypotenuse From Two Legs</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has legs <span set:html={rich('[[a = 12]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[b = 16]]')} /> cm. Find the hypotenuse first, then the inradius.</p>
  <h3>Problem 5: Area and Semiperimeter</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has legs <span set:html={rich('[[a = 15]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[b = 20]]')} /> cm and hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c = 25]]')} /> cm. Find the area and the semiperimeter, then find the inradius with <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{A}{s}]]')} />, and confirm it with the three-side formula.</p>
  <h3>Problem 6: Identify an Invalid Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>A student is given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 7]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[b = 10]]')} /> cm, and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 12]]')} /> cm and asked for the inradius. Show why these sides do not form a right triangle, and explain what the student should do instead of applying the formula.</p>
  <h3>Practice Problem Answers</h3>
  <p>Answer 1: <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{20+21-29}{2} = \\frac{12}{2} = 6]]')} /> cm. Check: <span set:html={rich('[[20^2+21^2 = 400+441 = 841 = 29^2]]')} />.</p>
